"""
SokoRadar capstone (Group 10) - data pipeline: raw files -> cleaned data -> charts -> county Excel panel.

Run:   python sokoradar_pipeline.py
       (or in Jupyter / Colab: upload this file, then run  %run sokoradar_pipeline.py)
       The raw data is embedded in this file: if there is no raw/ folder next to it, the data is unpacked to
       sokoradar_raw_data/ automatically. Results go to output/ next to the script.
Needs: Python 3.8+, pandas, numpy, openpyxl, matplotlib (missing ones are installed automatically with pip)

What it does, step by step
  1. Load the raw data (Ministry of Agriculture daily prices and market bulletin, WFP market prices, IMF world prices, CHIRPS/NDVI county climate, county list, national drivers).
  2. Clean outliers
       - MoA daily prices: zeros -> missing; a price more than 3x (or under 1/3 of) the 21-day rolling median of the
         same commodity -> removed.
       - WFP market prices: a price more than 3x (or under 1/3 of) the rolling median of the same market and
         commodity (5 reports) -> removed; more than 4x (or under 1/4 of) the median of all markets in a 5-month window -> removed;
         a county price more than 4x (or under 1/4 of) the national price -> removed.
     Every removal is written to the Cleaning_Log sheet.
  3. Handle missing values
       - MoA monthly price = average of the trading days in the month (10+ days = Observed, 1-9 = Thin).
       - Months with no trading days: filled with the MoA bulletin month-on-month trend (maize, beans, potatoes)
         or the commodity's average seasonal growth for that calendar month, nudged so the path lands on the next real
         price ("Filled"). Gaps at the ends are carried with seasonal growth.
       - 2006-2018 (before MoA data): WFP wholesale prices (median of 6 main markets) scaled to the MoA price level.
       - Items with no data before 2019/2020 (wheat, eggs, beef, goat meat, fish) are back-cast with the growth of a
         related series (world wheat price in KES; CPI) - flagged as back-cast.
  4. County disaggregation: county price = national price x county price ratio. The ratio comes from the county's own
     WFP prices if it has any, else the MoA county bulletin (2025-26), else the 3 nearest counties, else the county's
     average food price gap. Retail <-> wholesale via the markup measured where both exist.
  5. County drivers: rainfall, vegetation, drought/flood flags, distance to hubs and markets, transport cost, county
     events (pests, disease, drought emergencies, policy, elections); national drivers (oil, FX, diesel, inflation, CPI,
     ENSO) repeated for every county.
  6. Charts (output/figures) and the Excel workbook (output/SokoRadar_County_2006_2024.xlsx) + long CSV.
"""
import io, os, sys, json, math, re, zipfile, tarfile, base64, lzma, datetime, subprocess, traceback
